# SRG-APP-000392-AU-002300  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must be configured to electronically verify the DoW
# common access card (CAC) credential.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000392-AU-002300
group:           V-206507
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-001954
control_rev5:    IA-2

contribution:    Splunk can verify a presented card certificate rather than merely accept
                 it: requireClientCert makes the TLS layer validate the certificate against
                 the configured root CA, server.conf [sslConfig]
                 certificateStatusValidationMethod = crl turns on certificate status
                 (revocation) validation for every Splunk TLS channel including Splunk Web,
                 splunkd, and S2S, and certBasedUserAuthMethod binds the validated
                 certificate to a Splunk identity.

governs:         server.conf - Core server settings, including the certificates and protocols used to
                 protect management and replication traffic.
governs:         web.conf - Configures the Splunk Web interface.

check:           web.conf [settings] requireClientCert in true|True|TRUE|t|1|yes
check:           server.conf [sslConfig] certificateStatusValidationMethod equals crl
check:           web.conf [settings] certBasedUserAuthMethod in CommonName|EDIPI|PIV
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          server.conf [sslConfig] certificateStatusValidationMethod https://help.splunk.com/en/data-management/splunk-enterprise-admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/server.conf
vendor:          web.conf [settings] certBasedUserAuthMethod https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
vendor:          web.conf [settings] requireClientCert https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/manage-users-and-security/10.4/authenticate-into-splunk-enterprise-using-hardware/configure-splunk-enterprise-to-use-a-common-access-card-for-authentication
# --- end vendor verification

external:        Retrieval and refresh of revocation data - carried by a scheduled process
                 or the PKI's distribution mechanism; the product consumes PEM CRL files
                 placed in its CRL directory and does not fetch them itself.
external:        DoD PKI CRL distribution points and responders - carried by the
                 organization's PKI.
external:        Trust anchor file - supplied through web.conf sslRootCAPath or its
                 fallback to the server.conf setting of the same name, so an empty web.conf
                 value is not by itself a shortfall.
external:        Where PKI is terminated by an approved SSO proxy, certificate validation
                 is performed by that proxy and the application's share reduces to trusting
                 its assertion.

citation:        splunk-spec-files/server.conf.spec:714 ([sslConfig]), :1125
                 (certificateStatusValidationMethod = crl, only accepted value, default
                 empty); splunk-spec-files/web.conf.spec:133, :151
finding:         Splunk is not configured to verify a presented CAC/PIV certificate -
                 client certificate authentication is not required at the web tier and
                 certificate status validation is off (server.conf [sslConfig]
                 certificateStatusValidationMethod is unset), so a revoked card certificate
                 would still be honored; this determination is from the perspective of the
                 application layer alone.
